WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.320
My name is Sam Bates.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.770
I'm a koi pond specialist

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.253
at Grass Roots Pond and Garden,

00:00:15.253 --> 00:00:16.408
my family's business.

00:00:16.408 --> 00:00:17.790
And today I'm going to be showing you

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:21.070
how we do a deep cleaning on koi ponds.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.840
Koi ponds can get pretty dirty,

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.100
and this is for a number
of different reasons.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.991
One of which, at least here in Michigan,

00:00:26.991 --> 00:00:27.991
where we are from,

00:00:27.991 --> 00:00:29.720
we have a lot of deciduous trees

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.780
that drop leaves into the pond.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.390
They then break down.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.340
That organic matter can turn the water

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.648
either brown,

00:00:36.648 --> 00:00:38.700
or sometimes it helps make it green.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.360
This one over here is
what we call carpet algae.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.155
It's very, very difficult
to get off the bottom

00:00:45.155 --> 00:00:46.570
and the sides of the liner,

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.950
but it's actually beneficial to a pond

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.670
to have the carpet algae.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.780
This typically comes with age.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.760
The carpet algae can get
as long as shag carpet,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.790
and also it houses bacteria

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.380
and acts as another
biological filtration system

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.947
within your pond that you didn't even

00:01:01.947 --> 00:01:03.850
have to pay for.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.700
When we are removing koi from ponds,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.570
we use what's called a sock net.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:09.911
It's very silky smooth,

00:01:09.911 --> 00:01:12.063
so it's very delicate on the fish,

00:01:12.063 --> 00:01:13.480
and it has two open ends,

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.971
which makes it easy to hold,

00:01:14.971 --> 00:01:16.600
and the fish can't escape.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.150
And also, you can release the fish

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.660
through the bottom or
the backside of the net,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.010
so the fish really doesn't
undergo much stress

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:24.800
during the transportation process.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.230
When we're cleaning koi ponds,

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:29.500
we keep the koi, goldfish, frogs, and such

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.670
in separate collapsible containers.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.160
They hold 500 to 600 gallons of water.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.552
It leaves plenty of room
for the fish to hang out.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.150
Ponds that are overtaken with algae,

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.191
whether it be microscopic algae,

00:01:42.191 --> 00:01:43.830
which causes green water,

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.213
or string algae,

00:01:45.213 --> 00:01:47.363
getting a deep clean to the very bottom

00:01:47.363 --> 00:01:48.823
can be a good restart.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.830
This pond here represents
what we typically will find

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:57.230
when it comes springtime and
time to open up a koi pond.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.560
The water is green. That
is the suspended algae.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.220
The ammonia breakdown
from any organic matter,

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.062
whether that be, sadly,

00:02:05.062 --> 00:02:06.928
maybe a fish that passed away,

00:02:06.928 --> 00:02:09.060
or it can be tannins in the water,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.130
which leech from leaves,
pine cones, sticks,

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:13.310
anything like that,

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.418
and these things should be cleaned out

00:02:15.418 --> 00:02:17.518
really once a year.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.595
We use this hose connected to typically

00:02:21.595 --> 00:02:23.720
a 6,000-gallon-per-hour pump

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.680
that we submerge at the bottom of the pond

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.350
to remove the water.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.050
Now, where does the water go?

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.980
Well, it really depends on where we're at.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:32.800
Sometimes it goes to their yard,

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.740
sometimes it goes out to the road.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.640
It really all just depends.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.240
Some ponds only need
to get pumped out once.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.250
Those are the easiest.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.330
And then others, it can be anywhere

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.870
from two to a dozen times.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.300
When ponds are full of
gravel at the bottom,

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.400
you have to rinse in between the gravel,

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.700
and you'll often find
a lot of mud and debris

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.258
that's hidden to your eye,

00:02:53.258 --> 00:02:55.299
but we try to remove it just because

00:02:55.299 --> 00:02:57.270
it can still affect with water chemistry.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.136
Typically, when cleaning ponds,

00:02:59.136 --> 00:03:01.144
we find all sorts of stuff,

00:03:01.144 --> 00:03:03.030
but the regular things that we find

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.100
are bullfrogs, green frogs, toads,

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.300
lots of toads,

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:11.093
and we also find
dragonfly larvae, turtles.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:15.840
In all of this mucky stuff that we find

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.465
at the bottom of ponds,

00:03:17.465 --> 00:03:21.548
it ranges from really a
cocktail of tree leaves,

00:03:21.548 --> 00:03:23.870
sorts of needles and sticks,

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.030
and mostly fish waste.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:28.505
And it smells awful.

00:03:28.505 --> 00:03:30.263
There's really no other way to put it.

00:03:30.263 --> 00:03:34.080
The smell actually varies by pond.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.090
Some barely smell,

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:40.230
and then some make you
just want to run away,

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:41.747
but you have to go through it

00:03:41.747 --> 00:03:42.872
and you have to get it out.

00:03:42.872 --> 00:03:45.380
This pond was so large in surface area,

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.590
with a flat bottom,

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:50.170
to where we couldn't rinse
everything to one end.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:53.442
We're using a squeegee to actually remove

00:03:53.442 --> 00:03:56.070
all of the excess water and debris

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.260
that is stuck in the bottom of the pond

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:59.983
on their carpet algae.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.933
The rock, the liner can be
covered in string algae.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.570
If your lawnmower's aiming
in the wrong direction

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:13.960
and it's kicking grass
clippings into the pond,

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:17.670
or sometimes even sprays
like herbicides or pesticides

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.820
can raise the ammonia levels in the pond,

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.010
and that can cause just
a whole world of issues,

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:24.457
causing algae and even killing

00:04:24.457 --> 00:04:26.470
some of the pets within the system.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:27.840
So you get as close as you can

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.980
with the right bit on the power washer,

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.180
and you remove the algae,

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.030
then you rinse it to the bottom.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.710
And then we use a Shop-Vac

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.130
to clean up all of the
sludge and nasty stuff

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:38.360
that we left at the bottom,

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.270
as well as vacuuming out the skimmers

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.536
and the filters.

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.110
It's important to clean out the bottom

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:46.680
of the filtration system,

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.130
because this is where
all of the heavy sludge

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:50.696
and fish waste particularly

00:04:50.696 --> 00:04:53.504
like to set and stay stagnant.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:59.690
When refilling ponds
after a deep cleaning,

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:02.250
we typically just use
the client's garden hose,

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:04.550
and we will run it until
we have enough water

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:06.140
for the fish to be released.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:07.380
We add dechlorinator,

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.980
usually right at the beginning,

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.690
and we will aerate the pond

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:12.580
to ensure that the dechlorinator

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:15.100
has went thoroughly
through the existing water

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:16.570
before we release the fish.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.190
We make sure that the water
is splashing into the pond,

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:20.900
opposed to just filling from the bottom

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.700
and staying fairly stagnant.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:25.690
Creating those bubbles
helps push that chlorine

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:27.300
out of the system and gas off

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.450
to make it healthy for the fish.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:32.070
This bag of what looks to be dirty water

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:34.020
is actually beneficial bacteria

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.080
that helps break down fish waste

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.870
and helps with pond clarity

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.030
throughout the season.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:41.010
This is something that we add weekly,

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:42.757
and our clients often ask,

00:05:42.757 --> 00:05:44.965
"What is that bag of brown water,

00:05:44.965 --> 00:05:46.256
and what's in it?"

00:05:46.256 --> 00:05:48.090
All I can tell them is it's bacteria,

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:49.380
because that's my grandfather

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.860
and my father's secret recipe for years,

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.576
so I can't let you in on that.

